  "terminal_reason": "completed",
  "final_text": "The dashboard and the alert rule both exist, and the rule is enabled. Kibana shows the rule's last run as `ok`.\n\n**1. What the data holds.** The `Kibana Sample Data Logs` data view covers about 14,000 web requests. The time field is `timestamp`, and the data runs from early October to early December 2026, so some of it is dated in the future. Each request records:\n- response code (`response.keyword`): 12,832 were 200, 801 were 404 and 441 were 503;\n- `bytes` sent (503 responses send 0);\n- client IP;\n- requested URL and file extension;\n- source and destination country;\n- the client's operating system and user agent.\n\n**2. Dashboard \"EFF-c0a40462 Web logs overview\".** It has 7 panels, and its default time range is now-7d to now+60d so it includes the future-dated data:\n- Total requests (number)\n- Unique visitors (count of distinct client IPs)\n- Total bytes served\n- Requests over time, split by response code (bar chart)\n- Share of each response code (pie chart)\n- Top 10 requested URLs, with request count and bytes (table)\n- Top 10 source countries (bar chart)\n\nLink: http://localhost:15601/app/dashboards#/view/eff-c0a40462-web-logs-overview-e79c02f81e70\n\n**3. Rule \"EFF-c0a40462 5xx spike\"** (id `e670862d-1c6d-41ad-8ac6-068bff6673ee`). It runs every minute against `kibana_sample_data_logs`. It fires when more than 10 requests with a response code from 500 to 599 arrive within 5 minutes, using `timestamp` as the time field. I ran the same filter across all the data and it matched exactly the 441 responses with code 503.\n\nThe rule has no action attached, so when it fires the alert only shows up in Kibana and nobody is notified. If you want notifications, tell me where to send them (for example Slack or email) and I'll add a connector.",
